OTHER RESOLUTIONS

RESOLUTION ON STRATEGIC PLANNING

BE IT RESOLVED, That the National DSA, under a process and timetable to be established by the NPC, take as a major focus in the next period a thorough reassessment of (1) its basic strategy, (2) the realistic opportunities for the existing DSA to participate in the building of a progressive political force in the next few years, and (3) the appropriate organizational structures, processes, and tools for reaching its goals;

That everything be “on the table” except our commitment to socialist values and democratic procedures;

That the process begin with a realistic and unsentimental evaluation of (1) the size and diversity of the present activist core, (2) the viability of existing locals and commissions, (3) the contacts DSA has in other organizations and movements, and (4) our access to highly skilled analysts and high-profile spokespeople;

That the goal of the process be the development of one or more projects which can realistically be carried out by a group with the present size and characteristics of the DSA which will assist existing and potential progressive forces to form a political entity capable of offering an effective challenge to corporate power and free-market globalization.

RESOLUTION ON CORPORATIONS

The pernicious doctrine designating corporations as artificial persons has given them a degree of power exceeding that of natural persons. We should examine and pursue, in coalition with groups such as Programs on Corporations, Law and Democracy (POCLAD) and Alliance for Democracy, ways to reduce and eventually limit this power by defining persons only as living individuals.

RESOLUTION ON RADICAL YOUTH

WHEREAS, today’s activist youth constitute a bright hope for a reconstituted broad progressive movement, and an opening for the clear espousal of socialism within such movements;

THEREFORE, be it resolved that DSA nationally shall work actively and in a comradely manner with these radical youth.

RESOLUTION ON ENDORSEMENTS

DSA endorses the march on Washington for women’s right [April 25] to choose and the December 10 55th Anniversary of the UN Declaration of Human Rights, with specific focus on the right to organize.

[PDF page 1] Resolution #54 Title & Author “Criminal Justice and Decarceration” by Donald J., Seneca S., and Marina R. of Austin DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs $37,305 Appears to require priority campaign level support as exists now - 1/2 staffer. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $10,000 Campaign materials. Total Cost $51,781 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve ● ● Resolution would require staff time for supporting criminal justice campaign education, training, and development; estimated 40-80 hours per month Resolution would require expenditure of funds on educational and campaign materials; estimated $10,000 annually BE IT RESOLVED: 1) The DSA convention will charter a Police & Prison Abolition Working Group 2) The main signatories of this resolution will serve as the interim steering committee for this Working Group, with no more than 2 people per chapter; 3) Within its first four months of operation, the interim steering committee will ● create a survey to gather together DSA members who are already active in this work; staff will send this survey to all members; ● ● will convene monthly national calls that are open to all DSA members; will run an election for a steering committee before the end of 2019 4) The Working Group will gather together & create resources to support criminal justice organizing & education campaigns across the country including but not limited to: ● ● Trainings for power-mapping local criminal justice issues, which have parallel structures & which many chapters are well-situated to pressure; Common pool of issue primers & trainings, as well as canvassing material addressing shared criminal justice issues such as cash bail, judicial discretion, eviction protection from Justices of the Peace, police use of force rates, & costs of policing & incarceration; & for vetting & cultivating candidates to judicial races & running judicial electoral campaigns;

[PDF page 2] Background Information ● Trainings in deep canvassing focused on opposition/alternatives to mass incarceration; &, ● Coalition mapping & training for how to coordinate, assist & strengthen local abolitionist & reform groups 5) Staff will support the Working Group by: ● Creating a abolition.dsausa.org website where the WG will be able to update these trainings & resources themselves; ● Sending quarterly all-member emails from the Abolition WG ● Create a sectioned email list for those interested in the work of the WG 6) As a matter of our collective protection, DSA staff shall dedicate resources to tracking anti-protest laws across the country as part of their duty to provide training to all members; 7) The WG, in order to elevate the leadership of black, brown & indigenous people, will identify potential coalitions with existing community-led organizations toward the reduction of policing budgets, the expansion of civilian oversight of policing, & the promotion & funding of alternatives to carceral systems including offender-victim dialogue, conferencing, & sentencing circles; WHEREAS: 1. The American government wages war on working class communities, especially working class communities of color, to maintain the hierarchy of class domination and keep the working class segregated and weakened. The front line of that war are the police forces of cities across America, effectively taking away the streets from us through routine interactions that may end with people being put in cages or worse; 2. Mass incarceration effectively fractures entire communities of the working class; 3. America is unique in intensity and violence of policing among developed capitalist countries; 4. Police killed 1,147 people in 2017 and 1,164 people in 2018. Black people were 25% of those killed despite being only 13% of the population. (from MappingPoliceViolence.org); 5. Black people are twice as likely to be pulled over by police in traffic stops, and four times as likely to be searched (Baumgartner, Epp and Shoub interview); 6. Empirical research and the experience of communities consistently report heavy bias in policing, arrests, and sentencing, that lead to disproportionate arrests, heavier prison sentences, and continuous targeting of communities of color; 7. The for-profit prison industry and the militarization of police together have led to increasingly dire consequences for Black people and immigrants, who even as children face mortal threats at the hands of the state as well as corporate entities whose industry creates a business demand for incarcerated bodies; 8. Campaigns around courts, criminal justice reforms, and policing reform present powerful opportunities for DSA chapters to materially impact the lives of their communities;

[PDF page 3] 9. The consensus around policing and incarceration in the US is both historically contingent and momentarily fragile. The conditions that gave birth to mass incarceration no longer exist at present, and public support of incarceration is at an all time low. This presents a unique opportunity to reduce incarceration and dismantle the prison-industrial complex; 10. Broad coalitions of working class people exist across the country in the form of social movements against policing and incarceration.

[PDF page 1] Resolution #48 Title & Author “Candidate Litmus Test” by Ben F. (Phoenix DSA); Collin P . (Seattle DSA); Friendly amendments from the authors and Fainan L.(New York); Oren S. (NYC); Andy S. (Madison). (updated with friendly amendments 7/22) Budget Analysis Staff Costs $861 No additional up front costs. 12% Overhead $103 Direct Costs 0 Total Cost $964 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve No estimated costs, might involve staff time if staff validate candidate questionnaires. BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, that DSA’s National Electoral Committee (NEC), National Political Committee (NPC), and any national DSA executive body may only nationally endorse candidates who answer in the affirmative to the following questions (and thus that all Locals/Chapters seeking a national endorsement for a candidate must ask these questions of their candidate, and report the answers to the NEC and NPC): 1. Are you a democratic socialist? 2. Do you support Medicare for All bills that meet DSA’s 5 principles? These five principles stipulate that Medicare for All means the creation of a 1) single health insurance program, administered by the federal government, that 2) provides comprehensive coverage, including dental, vision, mental health, and pharmaceuticals, that 3) is free at the point of service, with no co-pays, fees, deductibles, or premiums. This program provides 4) universal coverage, regardless of immigration status, and 5) a jobs transition program for those affected by the transition to government-run health insurance. Do you commit to working for these principles at the national (and where appropriate, state) level?

[PDF page 2] 3. Do you support bills that increase workplace democracy, and specifically the following three ways of overturning key provisions of the Taft-Hartley Act of 1947: by instituting a card check system allowing workers to unionize by simple majority, by repealing the prohibitions on secondary strikes and recognition strikes, and by preventing states from passing so-called “right to work” laws? [For local candidates:] Do you support repealing relevant similar local laws where they exist (such as “right to work” legislation or bans on public sector workers striking)? 4. Do you support legislation for the provision of universal, tuition-free child care? 5. Do you support College for All, and specifically making public universities, colleges, and trade schools tuition-free and reducing student debt? [For local candidates:] Do you commit to fully funding public schools, and halting charter expansion and voucher programs? 6. Do you support legislation that drastically and rapidly decreases the size and scope of mass incarceration, including the demilitarization of local police forces, the re-enfranchisement of ex-felons, the legalization of marijuana, and the elimination of: for-profit prisons, ICE detention centers, mandatory minimums, the death penalty, and cash bail? 7. Do you support an end to immigration detention, the demilitarization of the border, and a path to citizenship for undocumented immigrants? 8. Do you support the Green New Deal, including rapid decarbonization of the economy, the creation of a federal jobs guarantee, and the creation of public financing structures to achieve public control over green development? 9. Do you oppose U.S. imperialism around the world through unequivocal opposition to U.S. military intervention and financial sanctions, as well as U.S. military, financial or other support for foreign actors in support of illegal occupation or regime change efforts? 10. Do you support progressive taxation measures, including higher taxes on corporations, millionaire and billionaire incomes and inheritances, and Wall Street speculation, and eliminating tax loopholes, breaks, and scams that benefit the capitalist class? Do you oppose regressive taxation measures, including regressive sales/consumption taxes, which disproportionately punish the working class? 11. Are you refusing contributions (direct and earmarked) from corporate PACs, business trade group PACs, or other business interests that conflict with the political program of DSA?

[PDF page 3] Background Information 12. Do you support democratizing the political process through reforms including, but not limited to, replacing corporate funding of elections with public funding of elections, ending voter suppression and gerrymandering, and implementing automatic voter registration for all adults over 18 years of age? 13. Do you support upholding Roe v Wade, repealing the Hyde Amendment, and fully funding Planned Parenthood, Title X, and other initiatives that protect women’s health, access to contraceptives and the availability of safe and legal abortion? 14. [In the 2020 electoral cycle:] Do you endorse Bernie Sanders’s 2020 presidential campaign? BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that if a Local endorses a candidate who does not receive a national endorsement the candidate will not be entitled to receive support in the form of financial donations, volunteer organization, media promotion, or otherwise, for that electoral campaign work from national DSA; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that local and regional bodies are encouraged to utilize these questions in questionnaires for all candidates seeking endorsement, in addition to whatever local criteria are already being used. WHEREAS, with the victories of DSA members like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, Rashida Tlaib, and Julia Salazar, DSA saw electoral success in 2018 unprecedented for a socialist organization since World War II; WHEREAS, building DSA’s political power through electoral campaigns is contingent on DSA-endorsed candidates advocating for and espousing positions consistent with DSA’s political goals once in office; WHEREAS, both DSA and DSA-endorsed candidates would benefit from a nationally recognized standard to which all endorsed candidates are held, such that voters can be certain that any candidate with a DSA endorsement will fight for, at the bare minimum, a clear program comprised of working-class demands; WHEREAS, defining this minimum program of litmus test issues is a key component of building and maintaining an independent political identity separate from the two-party system; WHEREAS, this minimum program must define the bare minimum required for a DSA endorsement, rather than a maximal program (everything DSA could possibly want from a candidate); WHEREAS, this minimum program ought to be supplemented by additional criteria as defined by regional and local bodies;

[PDF page 1] Resolution #49 Title & Author “PAC Spending for Nationally Endorsed DSA Candidates” by Alex N. & Dustin G. of Philly DSA. (updated with friendly amendments 7/12). Budget Analysis Staff Costs $74,610 Resolution misunderstands campaign finance law, but if amended and passed the work of administering Friendly amendments incorporated into this resolution resolved the campaign finance issues. The type of work in this resolution could be undertaken by the future Compliance Manager and Compliance Associate. Resolution still underestimates staff time needed - this would require hiring 1 full time staffer to assist the Development Director to fundraise. 12% Overhead $8,953 Direct Costs 0 Total Cost $83,563 Author’s Cost Estimate 80 hours of Development Director time over 1 year, $500,000 in DSA’s national PAC Resolve NOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED, that DSA commits to providing nationally endorsed electoral campaigns with at least $2,000 or the maximum legal limit, whichever is lower, in cash or in-kind contributions while maintaining appropriate firewalls between any coordinated and independent expenditures DSA may make on behalf of candidates and ballot measures; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that the NPC, in consultation with the Compliance Officer, be given discretion to make such cash and in-kind contributions through DSA’s federal PAC, a state or municipal PAC, or DSA’s treasury, depending on relevant campaign finance laws; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA’s Development Director form and chair a committee that includes the Compliance Officer and nine (9) DSA members, devoted to raising at least $500,000 for DSA’s PAC for the 2020 electoral cycle by soliciting PAC

[PDF page 2] Background Information contributions via phone and email from all of DSA’s members; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA’s new member signup and dues renewal forms include a field for making an optional, one-time contribution to DSA’s federal PAC defaulting to $27; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that nationally endorsed candidates be asked/encouraged to share all field, phone, fundraising, and text bank data with national DSA and all DSA chapters whose jurisdictions overlap the candidate’s district upon receiving a national endorsement and concomitant financial contributions to the extent allowable under local campaign finance laws; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that nationally endorsed candidates be asked/encouraged to use their campaigns to support the issue-based organizing of DSA chapters within their districts as a condition of receiving a national endorsement and concomitant financial contributions; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that national endorsements only be given to candidates who advance DSA’s national campaigns, including Bernie Sanders’s 2020 presidential campaign; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that national DSA’s campaign finance infrastructure only be used for spending on behalf of nationally endorsed candidates and ballot measures. WHEREAS, the electoral victories of DSA members like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, Rashida Tlaib, Julia Salazar, Elizabeth Fiedler, Summer Lee, and Sara Innamorato raise the profile of democratic socialism; WHEREAS, electoral campaigns offer approachable organizing and skills-building opportunities for DSA members; WHEREAS, national endorsements for DSA candidates are a means of vetting the viability of local candidates and providing them with further exposure; WHEREAS, national endorsements ought to benefit both DSA and nationally endorsed candidates more than they do; WHEREAS, the relative insignificance of national endorsements is an effect of DSA’s underfunded and underutilized electoral infrastructure;

[PDF page 3] WHEREAS, a viable long-term strategy for democratic socialist candidates will require a durable fundraising and coordination mechanism to ensure that Locals receive the necessary support for their nationally endorsed candidates; WHEREAS, DSA could leverage its 65,000 strong membership to improve this infrastructure to lend weight to our national endorsements, to provide genuine assistance to our nationally endorsed candidates, and thus to develop an apparatus to hold nationally endorsed candidates accountable to the organization; WHEREAS, DSA’s separate segregated fund (PAC) may be used to spend money in federal and state-level races; WHEREAS, in 2018 DSA PAC’s only disbursements were spent on bank fees; WHEREAS, a well-funded and coordinated strategy for utilizing the DSA PAC would ease the burden on Local chapters which could rely on the national organization for funds without the need to independently organize ad hoc fundraising;

[PDF page 1] Resolution #40 Title & Author “Housing as a Human Right” by Donald J. and Stephanie T . of Austin DSA. Staff Costs $37,305 Appears to be priority campaign - 1/2 staffer. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $10,000 Campaign materials. Total Cost $51,781 Author’s Cost Estimate ● ● Resolution would require staff time for supporting a national and local Socialist Housing Working Groups and Committees, including education, training, and campaign development; estimated 40-80 hours per month Resolution would require expenditure of funds on educational and campaign materials; estimated $10,000 annually Resolve BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED: 1. DSA declares that fair, safe, stable, accessible, and affordable housing is a human right; 2. DSA shall dedicate resources to support tenant organizing and education campaigns in cities across the country committed to the following: a) Promoting the decommodification of housing through expanding publicly-owned housing, social housing funds, and social housing supply; b) Developing solidarity networks dedicated to defending and expanding renters’ rights and supporting grassroots campaigns for tenant rights and empowerment; c) Resisting tenant evictions, slum conditions, exploitation, discrimination, gentrification, and displacement through collaboration with democratic renters’ organizations and helping organize tenant unions where they are not already present; d) Abolishing statutes and practices that criminalize homelessness and create barriers to housing such as criminal and eviction background checks; and, e) Defending, establishing, and expanding rent control. 3. DSA shall establish a Socialist Housing Working Group to help coordinate national and inter-local housing justice campaigns, coordinate and promote education on housing policy and socialist housing strategies, and support the creation or expansion of socialist housing local working groups, campaigns, and projects; 4. DSA shall require that any candidate seeking DSA electoral support should agree to the following, as applicable: f) Declare and advance Housing as a Human Right;

[PDF page 2] Background Information g) Fund and build more public housing, social housing, rent-controlled housing, income-restricted housing, cooperative housing, transit-accessible housing, sustainable housing, accessible housing, and integrated housing; h) Fight for the elimination of evictions, displacement, gentrification, homelessness, criminalization of homelessness, discrimination based on class, race, country of origin, gender, sex, familial status, and LGBTQ status, discrimination based on criminal and eviction background, discrimination based on source of income (e.g., Housing Choice Vouchers), and sprawl; i) Fight for rent control, tenants rights and tenant organizing, funding for affordable housing, housing and services for those experiencing homelessness, sustainable development, and non-exclusionary zoning; and j) Oppose measures that reduce or are contrary to any of the above. WHEREAS: 5. Housing is a human right essential to health, economic stability, and basic existence. 6. The working class will always face a housing crisis under capitalism and a private property regime. 7. The commodification and financialization of housing has created a crisis of affordability, exploitation, gentrification, and displacement. Housing costs are among the greatest direct burdens on the working class in cities across the United States; 8. The privatized system of American housing has failed to provide for the essential need of stable shelter for millions, leaving them debt burdened, in unsafe conditions, and increasingly precariously housed or homeless; 9. American housing policy has been crafted to reinforce segregation by race and class and to disempower and displace working class communities, especially those of color; 10. Public housing is deeply underfunded, with an estimated $40 billion dollars in public housing capital repair backlog and additional operational funding needed; 11. The chronic and growing unaffordability of American housing is exacerbating homelessness that the private market will never remedy, especially hurting economically-burdened families, the physically and mentally differently abled, and people forced to remain in abusive accommodations, in particular non-men; 12. Social housing models, especially publicly-owned housing, rent-controlled housing, housing cooperatives, and land trusts have proven effective at serving the needs of people above profit;

[PDF page 3] 13. The People’s Policy Project has proposed a strategy for scaling up social housing by building 10 million new public housing units, built carbon neutral according to ecological principles, across the country (Link Here: Social Housing in the United States); and, 14. Compact, ecologically built housing with transit access can be a core industry to employ hundreds of thousands of unionized workers through a Green New Deal and is an essential piece of any comprehensive strategy to avert climate catastrophe in the coming decade.

[PDF page 1] Resolution #25 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Childcare” by Laura C. of Chicago DSA; Bria D. of Boston DSA; Logan Y. of New Orleans DSA. Staff Costs $37,305 National campaign - 1/2 time staffer 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $20,000 Campaign materials. Total Cost $61,781 Authors’ Cost Estimate $20,000, staff time for coordinating campaign work in Resolved #1 and project work in coordination with SFWG in Resolved #2. Resolve Be it therefore resolved: 1. That DSA prioritize the fight for guaranteed paid parental leave and public universal child care and preschool, and dedicate resources, including staff time and $10,000 over the next two years, to develop a national campaign for federal, state, and local legislation guaranteeing paid parental leave and free, high-quality child care and preschool for all children; 2. That DSA require endorsed candidates to support guaranteed paid parental leave and public universal child care and preschool and encourage candidates and elected officials to develop universal (not means-tested) policies to that effect; 3. That DSA’s National Labor Commission or DSA members active in labor organizing and the Socialist Feminist Working Group or a sub-committee thereof collaborate to develop a feminist labor strategy to support efforts by domestic and child care workers to organize and improve their wages and working conditions; 4. That DSA dedicate national resources, including staff time and $10,000 to be spent at the direction of the Socialist Feminist Working Group in coordination with the NPC and national staff as well as members that have developed existing child watch programs, to: a. develop or modify and disseminate child watch materials for chapters, starting with the adaptation of existing chapter initiatives, and a survey of chapters to identify their needs and preferences in child watch programming, b. train members to provide child watch services at chapter meetings and events while underlining the need for cis men to readily take up this work, c. help chapters navigate statewide requirements for

[PDF page 2] Background Information providing such services, and d. make child watch programs as inclusive as possible of disabled children and children with other needs; 5. That the Socialist Feminist Working Group or a sub-committee thereof will establish a timeline and milestones for child watch program progress in coordination with the NPC and national staff, with the goal of working towards at least 75% of chapters having at least one member trained in child watch programming, and 50% of chapters having a regular child watch program, providing child watch for at least every chapter-wide meeting, by August 1, 2020. WHEREAS, 1. Our socialism is feminist, pro-worker, and dedicated to fighting against the burdens of capitalism for all parents and caregivers, particularly for women of the working class, who provide disparate amounts of reproductive labor, including child care; 2. The United States is one of the only countries in the world that does not require workplaces to offer paid parental leave to workers; 3. Low wages require most parents and caregivers to work full-time or more than full-time outside the home, causing them to rely on paid care for their children, or to rely upon the uncompensated labor of extended relatives or community members who may already be burdened by multiple obligations under capitalism; 4. Daycare and other forms of child care are expensive, costing more than in-state tuition at a four-year public college in 33 states, and are an undue burden on the working class; 5. Child care workers often receive low wages, are deeply exploited by difficult working conditions, are disproportionately women of color or immigrants, may not be eligible to unionize under the NLRA, often work in the informal sector, and often struggle to find affordable or adequate care for their own children while they care for the children of others; 6. Domestic and child care work is a key, largely unorganized sector of the economy, related to other female-dominated sectors of reproductive labor such as nursing and teaching; 7. The capitalist economy relies upon the unpaid or underpaid reproductive labor of people who can become pregnant and people who take on caregiving roles to reproduce its workforce; 8. Providing free, high-quality universal child care and preschool would reduce the reliance of working parents on their workplaces for wages and child care benefits, thus shoring up worker power; 9. Socialism recognizes that childrearing should be a communal undertaking, and that by providing welcoming and supportive spaces for children, we can support both those children’s own social, political, and emotional development, along with ameliorating their primary caregivers’ alienation and atomization under capitalism;

[PDF page 3] 10. Our own organizing spaces must be inclusive, accessible, and welcoming to people of all ages, including families with children, and we must begin educating and valuing the input of the next generation of Democratic Socialists. Amendment #4 on Resolution #25 Title & Authors Diego) “ Adding a Survey & Gender Equality to Resolution #25” by Shauna M (San Insert into Section 4(a) “and a survey of chapters to identify their needs and preferences in child watch programming,” Insert into Section 4(b) Final language of Section 4 “while underlining the need for cis men to readily take up this work” That DSA dedicate national resources, including staff time and $10,000 to be spent at the direction of the Socialist Feminist Working Group in coordination with the NPC and national staff as well as members that have developed existing child watch programs, to: a. develop or modify and disseminate child watch materials for chapters, starting with the adaptation of existing chapter initiatives, and a survey of chapters to identify their needs and preferences in child watch programming, b. train members to provide child watch services at chapter meetings and events while underlining the need for cis men to readily take up this work, c. help chapters navigate statewide requirements for providing such services, and d. make child watch programs as inclusive as possible of disabled children and children with other needs;

[PDF page 1] Resolution #60 Title & Author “Reassert our Commitment to Training and Leadership Development” by Ravi A. of Long Beach DSA; Nan K. of North Texas DSA: Erika P . of Lincoln NB DSA. Updated with friendly amendments 7/22/2019. Budget Analysis Staff Costs $6,457 Some of the work coordinating would be done by the new Conventions and Conferences Coordinator Michael, but additional staff time would be required to process significant reimbursements for a fund of $150,000. 12% Overhead $775 Direct Costs $150,000 Cost of fund. Total Cost $157,231 Author’s Cost Estimate Because national DSA has spent already approximately $320,000 on various forms of training, this ought to be significantly cheaper, because it is not a directly managed set of events from national DSA. However, the costs to the individual chapters for their own events should be significantly alleviated by a national fund. We recommend that the NPC budget $150,000 in the first year, which is equal to $18,750 for eight regional events. Resolve Be it resolved, the DSA National Convention reasserts its commitment to a robust member-led training program and establishes the member-led National Training Committee to replace the National Training Team, which staff closed in Spring 2018; Be it further resolved, the authors and main signatories of this resolution will form an interim steering committee for this Working Group, with no more than 2 people per chapter;

[PDF page 2] Be it further resolved, the DSA Organizing Director will be an ex officio member of the Training Team SC and staff organizers ex officio members of the Team itself; neither will be expected to staff the committee in terms of logistics, agenda setting or facilitation; Be it further resolved, within its first four months of operation, the interim steering committee will: · Survey chapters and members on their programs, skills and needs; · Staff will send this survey to all members and share through its usual channels with chapter leaders; · The interim SC will analyze and collate the results in a report to the NPC and to the membership; · Initiate monthly national calls that are open to all DSA members; · Conduct an election for a steering committee before the end of 2019 open to all DSA members as candidates and as voters. Be it further resolved, the Committee is charged with developing a National Training Strategy, to be approved by the NPC to cover the following items and others as needed: · Support a community of practice and network of support for those engaged in relevant work across DSA; · Recruit experienced educators to work with staff and chapter leaders to assess the needs of members, develop lesson plans, curriculums and other tools to bring together political education and skills trainings; · Develop and deliver regular national trainings via zoom and, where feasible, in person on organizing skills; · Develop and maintain a library of training materials from diverse sources and make these resources available to all members; · Create and maintain a process for national to support chapter, state and regional training events with regular zoom calls such as train-the-trainer on various campaigns (e.g. Brake Lights Clinic, Expose Fake Clinics) as well as Socialism 101, Organizing Conversations, Power Mapping and others; · Support staff organizers by being a resource for training materials and trainers for their chapter visits and other responsibilities, in particular with new OCs and chapters;

[PDF page 3] Background · Work with staff and others to develop templates for fundraising, event planning, and other logistical aspects of hosting a training event. Be it further resolved, the Committee will provide quarterly updates to the NPC and membership on its activities and plans for the future. Be it further resolved, operations Staff will provide ongoing support the Training Team by: · Creating a training.dsausa.org website where the team will be able to update these trainings and resources themselves; · Sending at least one quarterly all-member email from the Training Team; · Creating a sectioned email list for those interested in the ongoing work of the team that the team will be able to contact directly. Note: any CRM will allow people to send emails without seeing contact information and other information that requires a higher level of security. Be it finally resolved, by planning ahead for in person events and using best practices in online pedagogy, the establishment of this Committee will reduce the costs to national DSA from approximately $320,000 on various forms of trainings and events to $150,000 in the first year, which is equal to $18,750 for eight regional events. Whereas DSA passed a comprehensive training resolution at our 2017 Convention and, by 2019, national had trained 500 chapter leaders rather than the thousands of members as envisioned in the 2017 training resolution; Whereas engaging our existing membership and building out solid internal infrastructure is essential to double DSA’s membership to over 100,000 members, and double membership again after that. Whereas we must train core organizers to deliberately bring people into the organization and to develop members from inactive donors to strong political leaders and core organizers; Whereas our staff organizers deserve a deep and wide bench of vetted, skilled members to support the rapid skilling up of our wider membership;

[PDF page 4] Whereas the division of organizing skills, strategic planning and political education is a false one and we believe that all people have skills, experiences and capacities from which we can all learn; Whereas dozens of chapters have developed and conducted alternate training and conference events and national remains largely unaware of these events and the capacities of our membership; Whereas, in January 2018, the NPC budgeted $50,000 for regional training events in place of the National Activist Conference required by our bylaws but staff instead spent only $8,029 to train 500 chapter leaders; Whereas the Spring 2019 Regional preconventions were budgeted at $276,800 due to their last minute scheduling; and Whereas we spent only $44,075 for the YDSA Winter & Summer conferences;

[PDF page 1] Resolution #55 Title & Author “Resolution on Grassroots Fundraising and Small Chapter Growth” by Nate K. of Metro Atlanta DSA; Travis D. of Knoxville DSA; Sam L. of NYC DSA.; Chris O. of St. Louis DSA; Riley W. of Olympia DSA; Michelle F. of Snohomish County DSA; Amy P . of Chicago DSA. (updated with friendly amendments 7/10) Budget Analysis Staff Costs $37,305 Some of this work is already planned with the hire of our new Tech and Data Manager Rebecca, Membership Services Associate Elizabeth, and Organizing Director Glenn, in addition to Development Director Alison. But significant staff time would be required to develop guide, administer chapter mentor program, and coordinate the whole project - estimated 1/2 time staffer more. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $0 Printing is already in national budget. Dues sharing costs not listed because resolution says they should be increased but not by how much. Total Cost $41,781 Author’s Cost Estimate Staff would work with the National Tech Committee to develop a monthly dues drive webpage, as well as set up payment processor support for ACH Direct Debit, potentially involving a redesign of the main dues page as well. Staff would create training materials and graphics for locals to use in local dues drives. Staff would spend time talking to locals about the dues drive and encouraging them to participate. Staff would work with volunteers to create an organizing committee / small chapter organizing manual and training materials for regional conferences. Staff would work with volunteers to establish a national mentorship program. This resolution establishes that chapters shall receive double the dues-share on their first 50 members. However, it does not set a firm dues-share rate. The intent is that as a part of this plan, the NPC should work with staff in committee to create cost-estimates for various dues-share rates, and then make a final proposal for a dues share increase. Resolve

[PDF page 2] Background Information BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, that the National Political Committee, in consultation with staff, chapter leaders, and members, shall devise and implement a plan by February 1st, 2020 to support a national dues drive; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that this plan should focus on working with locals to engage in local dues drives and grassroots fundraising by signing up local active membership for monthly dues; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that as a part of this plan DSA shall make public a web page(s) specifically for people to sign up for membership with monthly dues, in which a person may indicate their chapter affiliation, choose a monthly dues amount, and choose a payment method with electronic funds transfer provided as an option; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that all incorporated DSA chapters shall receive dues-sharing according to the following rates: 30% of the average monthly dues contribution for the first 50 monthly- dues-paying members; 25% for monthly-dues-paying members 51-200; and 20% for any monthly-dues-paying members exceeding 200. BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA shall publish an organizing committee/small chapter organizing manual that includes guidance for conducting dues drives, incorporating as a DSA chapter, and opening bank account no later than February 1st, 2020; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that special programming for organizing committees and small chapters generally and rural organizing committees and chapters specifically be provided at regional conferences; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA shall revive the national mentorship program and create a large chapter-OC/small chapter buddy program whereby volunteer mentors work with national staff to provide regular advice and support to remote organizing committees and small chapters; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that such mentorship and buddy programs should especially foster relationships between locals within the same state, especially to provide liaison support for assistance in compliance with relevant rules, laws, and regulations; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that the convention calls on each DSA local to endeavor to participate in the dues drive to build a strong, sustainable national organization with well resourced locals; WHEREAS, in order to fund the organization's staff and resources while still representing working class people, DSA engages in grassroots fundraising and solicits low-dollar donations;

[PDF page 3] WHEREAS, DSA benefits greatly from being inclusive of low-income members by offering a wide range of dues options and by waiving dues requirements when a member is unable to pay; WHEREAS, the implementation of a monthly dues system, dues splitting, and electronic funds transfers were all authorized at the 2017 DSA Convention; WHEREAS, most of DSA's members make one-time or annual dues payments, but many locals have active membership who are willing to make larger contributions through monthly dues; WHEREAS, DSA’s ambitious organizing goals require financial and staff support for strong locals and a strong national organization; WHEREAS, DSA should prioritize continued growth and devote additional resources to supporting new chapters; Amendment #28 to Resolution #55 Title & Author “ Amendment on Unincorporated Chapters” by Chris O. (St. Louis DSA), Riley W.P . (Olympia DSA), Michelle F. (Snohomish County DSA), Amy P . (Chicago DSA) Insert between 4th & 5th Resolve BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, any chapter that is unincorporated shall receive their dues share allocations through a method to be determined by the National Office until the date of their incorporation or for up to two years, whichever occurs first. For this purpose, the National Office should consider a combination of: ● ● Prepaid debit cards to be distributed to chapter treasurers Credit towards a catalog of DSA supplies, such as banners, buttons, literature, stickers, etc. ● Fiscal sponsorships of unincorporated chapters by nearby incorporated chapters

Resolution #41
Title & Author
“Take the Fossil Fuel Energy Companies into Democratic Public Ownership Resolution for the DSA National Convention” by Philip L., Harris L., Ramy K., and Stuart S. of Seattle DSA



Resolve

Be it resolved, as part of supporting and campaigning for the Green New Deal, DSA will advocate taking the major fossil fuel energy companies into democratic public ownership and to reallocate their massive resources into clean, renewable energy such as solar, wind, and geothermal;
Be it further resolved, DSA demands that before any compensation is paid in the process of taking those corporations into democratic public ownership, the fossil fuel companies must be put on trial for the untold damage they have caused the planet and humanity and their systematic suppression of evidence of climate change. They must be held accountable, criminally and financially. Compensation should go to those with proven need, such as working-class and middle-class small shareholders;
Be it further resolved, that DSA urges all its members elected to federal, state, and local political office to promote these demands; and
Be it finally resolved, that DSA will promote these demands as part of its independent Democratic Socialists for Bernie campaign.


Background Information
WHEREAS Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez and others are proposing a Green New Deal that includes eliminating greenhouse gas emissions in the US by 2030, ensuring a “just transition” for the communities and workers currently employed by or dependent on the fossil fuel industry, and implementing major reforms like universal health care, affordable housing, and food security for all, especially communities of color;
WHEREAS we recognize that the Green New Deal is incompatible with the power that fossil fuel capitalists have over our country and government, capitalists who are directly threatening the lives of billions of people and all life on earth;
WHEREAS the major fossil fuel energy companies have amassed some of the greatest accumulation of power and capital in history which they will deploy to block the passage of a robust Green New Deal;
WHEREAS “[J]ust 100 companies have been the source of more than 70% of the world’s greenhouse gas emissions since 1988,” as The Guardian reported (July 10, 2017);
WHEREAS Exxon’s own research in the 1970s revealed that fossil fuels contribute to climate change, and then oil companies intentionally suppressed this finding for decades;
WHEREAS the Koch brothers are the owners of the second largest privately-owned corporation in the US, which made a large part of its profits through fossil fuels and they buy politicians like other people buy groceries;
WHEREAS HuffPost reports: “The nine Democrats selected by House Speaker Nancy Pelosi (D-Calif.) to serve on the Select Committee on the Climate Crisis received a combined $198,000 from fossil fuel industry political action committees in the 2018 election cycle” (February 7, 2019);
WHEREAS the most aggressive fossil fuel industry groups drove the US into two wars in Iraq, propping up the reactionary Saudi monarchy over decades, and attempting or carrying out coups in Venezuela, Iran, and countless other countries;
WHEREAS we cannot control what we do not own;
